Dear Roger,

I've taken out the nine statements we stopped at on 10 July and put them in W=
hat would bring the nine back, with the passage or record I'd need to see bef=
ore using each one again. The chapter now works from the 54 references I chec=
ked in the paper copies. I've kept their numbering, so you can still use your=
 marked copy to find them.

The PDF is v0.3 for a read through. I've attached the CSV separately so you c=
an go back from any of the 63 old notes to the passage we checked or, for the=
 nine we took out, to the author's page and the candidate shelfmark.

Could you look particularly at the opening on who did the society's work and =
at the account of visits in =C2=A73? The circular now carries just the secret=
ary's and treasurer's duties and the instruction about sending money for seve=
ral members. On visits, I'd like to know whether the distinction comes throug=
h on a first reading: in the evening attendant's letter of 5 December 1865 th=
e newspaper and book have two lines between them, but the reader came once. T=
hat lets us read those entries without assuming the rest of the register was =
kept the same way.

Many thanks,
Judith

